# Vercel MCP / CLI guardrails

## Production truth (until user confirms cutover)

- **`navigate-tech-hub-6st3`** is the working production deploy (`frontend` root, Next.js).
- **`navigate-tech-hub`** is the target canonical hostname; currently misconfigured (repo root, wrong framework).
- Do not treat the short-name project as production-ready until a successful deploy is verified.

## Never without explicit user approval in the same task

- Delete or rename any Vercel project.
- Disconnect Git or change production branch / repo link.
- Production rollback, promote old deployment, or alias swap.
- Add, remove, or overwrite environment variables (no bulk copy; never log or commit secret values).
- Change root directory, framework preset, build/install commands, or Node version.
- `deploy_to_vercel`, `vercel deploy --prod`, or any production deploy.
- Domains, DNS, redirects at project level, or domain purchase.
- Write changes on **`navigate-tech-hub-6st3`** except read-only inspection.

## Default mode

- **Read-only first:** `list_projects`, `get_project`, `list_deployments`, `get_deployment`, build/runtime logs, `search_vercel_documentation`.
- Propose a numbered checklist; execute **one write step** only after the user says to proceed (e.g. “do step 2” or “go ahead”).
- If a deploy fails, diagnose only—do not chain unapproved fixes.

## Consolidation write target

- Configuration writes apply only to **`navigate-tech-hub`** unless the user explicitly authorizes retiring `6st3`.
- Repo changes (e.g. `frontend/vercel.json` host redirect) follow normal git workflow; user must approve commits.

## MCP vs CLI

- Vercel MCP does **not** expose project PATCH, env CRUD, or git disconnect—use CLI only when approved, with the same guardrails above.

## Official MCP endpoint

- Use `https://mcp.vercel.com` in `.cursor/mcp.json` (not `mcp-vercel.com`).
